          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.611,
            0.068,
            0.815,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "The World of Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "51"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.111,
            0.885,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "For example, to represent $\\frac{3}{4},$ divide the interval between 0 and 1 into four equal parts and move three parts to the right from 0."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.175,
            0.732,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.464,
                0.236,
                0.534,
                0.255
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.261,
            0.886,
            0.357
          ],
          "content": "Even fractions greater than 1 can be located on the number line in this way. For example, ${ \\frac { 9 } { 4 } } = 2 { \\frac { 1 } { 4 } }$ so it lies between 2 and 3. We divide the interval between 2 and 3 into four equal parts and move one part to the right of 2. See Fig. 3.6."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.374,
            0.844,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.464,
                0.437,
                0.535,
                0.455
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.6"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.485,
            0.399,
            0.509
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.522,
            0.586,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "Try and represent $\\frac{8}{5}  and  -\\frac{7}{4}$ on a number line."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.475,
            0.879,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.228,
                0.662,
                0.77,
                0.681
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.7: Some integers and rational numbers on a number line"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.703,
            0.581,
            0.724
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Absolute value of a rational number"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.733,
            0.883,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": "The **absolute value** of a rational number $X ,$ written as $| x |$ , represents its distance from 0 on the number line."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.775,
            0.731,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "**Example 1:** $\\left| \\frac{5}{3} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}, \\left| -\\frac{5}{3} \\right|$ is also equal to $\\frac { 5 } { 3 }$ and $| 0 | = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.825,
            0.886,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the absolute value of a positive number is the number itself. The absolute value of a negative number is its positive value. Therefore, the absolute value of any rational number is always non-negative, that is, $| x | \\geq 0$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
